## Summary: Goose as an Agentic Harness

### Strengths:
✓ **Clean state machine abstraction** — Composable, testable agent operations  
✓ **MCP-first design** — Universal tool ecosystem, extensible  
✓ **Local-first execution** — Privacy-preserving, offline capable  
✓ **Multiple LLM support** — 15+ providers, unified interface  
✓ **Rich permission model** — Three-layer approval (rules, LLM, human)  
✓ **Session persistence** — SQLite-backed, resumable workflows  
✓ **Recipe system** — Templated multi-turn workflows  
✓ **Production-ready** — OpenTelemetry, structured logging, error handling  

### Weaknesses:
✗ **Limited multi-agent orchestration** — Max depth 2, no hierarchies  
✗ **No RAG/memory** — Session-scoped only  
✗ **Weak planning** — Linear recipes, no auto-adaptation  
✗ **Distributed execution** — Single-machine only  
✗ **API complexity** — Embedded HTTP, manual OpenAPI  
✗ **Extension security** — Full user privileges, best-effort malware detection  

### Best Use Cases:
- **Local AI coding assistant** — CLI + Desktop for developers
- **Workflow automation** — Recipes for repetitive tasks
- **Research & analysis** — Long-form reasoning with tool support
- **Desktop automation** — Computer control via MCP
- **Multi-provider switching** — Works with any LLM via config

### Not Suitable For:
- Multi-agent swarms or hierarchies
- Real-time systems (latency-sensitive)
- Untrusted code execution (sandboxing limited)
- High-throughput production APIs (not designed for it)
- Closed-loop autonomous loops (no safety guardrails for pure autonomy)

